In our geometrically ordered society the moral status of any act is fixed by the pure forms it realizes or corrupts. Humiliation deliberately warps bodies and roles into irregular, non-Euclidean contortions that violate the angular clarity and curved harmony required of every citizen; such deformation is intrinsically vicious whether or not the participants “agree.” Payment and secrecy are irrelevant secondary accidents that cannot restore the broken symmetry.  

Thus the decisive considerations are the inviolable geometric limits on exchange and the irrelevance of mere consent once form has been desecrated.

